.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/@eslint/object-schema Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "ObjectSchema Package"
.PP
.SH "Overview"
.PP
A JavaScript object merge/validation utility where you can define a different merge and validation strategy for each key\&. This is helpful when you need to validate complex data structures and then merge them in a way that is more complex than \fRObject\&.assign()\fP\&. This is used in the \fR\fR@eslint/config-array\fP\fP package but can also be used on its own\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
For Node\&.js and compatible runtimes:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install @eslint/object\-schema
# or
yarn add @eslint/object\-schema
# or
pnpm install @eslint/object\-schema
# or
bun install @eslint/object\-schema
.fi
.PP

.PP
For Deno:

.PP
.PP
.nf
deno add @eslint/object\-schema
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
Import the \fRObjectSchema\fP constructor:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// using ESM
import { ObjectSchema } from "@eslint/object\-schema";

// using CommonJS
const { ObjectSchema } = require("@eslint/object\-schema");

const schema = new ObjectSchema({
    // define a definition for the "downloads" key
    downloads: {
        required: true,
        merge(value1, value2) {
            return value1 + value2;
        },
        validate(value) {
            if (typeof value !== "number") {
                throw new Error("Expected downloads to be a number\&.");
            }
        },
    },

    // define a strategy for the "versions" key
    version: {
        required: true,
        merge(value1, value2) {
            return value1\&.concat(value2);
        },
        validate(value) {
            if (!Array\&.isArray(value)) {
                throw new Error("Expected versions to be an array\&.");
            }
        },
    },
});

const record1 = {
    downloads: 25,
    versions: ["v1\&.0\&.0", "v1\&.1\&.0", "v1\&.2\&.0"],
};

const record2 = {
    downloads: 125,
    versions: ["v2\&.0\&.0", "v2\&.1\&.0", "v3\&.0\&.0"],
};

// make sure the records are valid
schema\&.validate(record1);
schema\&.validate(record2);

// merge together (schema\&.merge() accepts any number of objects)
const result = schema\&.merge(record1, record2);

// result looks like this:

const result = {
    downloads: 75,
    versions: ["v1\&.0\&.0", "v1\&.1\&.0", "v1\&.2\&.0", "v2\&.0\&.0", "v2\&.1\&.0", "v3\&.0\&.0"],
};
.fi
.PP
.SH "Tips and Tricks"
.PP
.SS "Named merge strategies"
Instead of specifying a \fRmerge()\fP method, you can specify one of the following strings to use a default merge strategy:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"assign"\fP - use \fRObject\&.assign()\fP to merge the two values into one object\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"overwrite"\fP - the second value always replaces the first\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"replace"\fP - the second value replaces the first if the second is not \fRundefined\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const schema = new ObjectSchema({
    name: {
        merge: "replace",
        validate() {},
    },
});
.fi
.PP
.SS "Named validation strategies"
Instead of specifying a \fRvalidate()\fP method, you can specify one of the following strings to use a default validation strategy:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"array"\fP - value must be an array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"boolean"\fP - value must be a boolean\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"number"\fP - value must be a number\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"object"\fP - value must be an object\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"object?"\fP - value must be an object or null\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"string"\fP - value must be a string\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR"string!"\fP - value must be a non-empty string\&.
.PP

.PP
For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const schema = new ObjectSchema({
    name: {
        merge: "replace",
        validate: "string",
    },
});
.fi
.PP
.SS "Subschemas"
If you are defining a key that is, itself, an object, you can simplify the process by using a subschema\&. Instead of defining \fRmerge()\fP and \fRvalidate()\fP, assign a \fRschema\fP key that contains a schema definition, like this:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const schema = new ObjectSchema({
    name: {
        schema: {
            first: {
                merge: "replace",
                validate: "string",
            },
            last: {
                merge: "replace",
                validate: "string",
            },
        },
    },
});

schema\&.validate({
    name: {
        first: "n",
        last: "z",
    },
});
.fi
.PP
.SS "Remove Keys During Merge"
If the merge strategy for a key returns \fRundefined\fP, then the key will not appear in the final object\&. For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const schema = new ObjectSchema({
    date: {
        merge() {
            return undefined;
        },
        validate(value) {
            Date\&.parse(value); // throws an error when invalid
        },
    },
});

const object1 = { date: "5/5/2005" };
const object2 = { date: "6/6/2006" };

const result = schema\&.merge(object1, object2);

console\&.log("date" in result); // false
.fi
.PP
.SS "Requiring Another Key Be Present"
If you'd like the presence of one key to require the presence of another key, you can use the \fRrequires\fP property to specify an array of other properties that any key requires\&. For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const schema = new ObjectSchema();

const schema = new ObjectSchema({
    date: {
        merge() {
            return undefined;
        },
        validate(value) {
            Date\&.parse(value); // throws an error when invalid
        },
    },
    time: {
        requires: ["date"],
        merge(first, second) {
            return second;
        },
        validate(value) {
            // \&.\&.\&.
        },
    },
});

// throws error: Key "time" requires keys "date"
schema\&.validate({
    time: "13:45",
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
In this example, even though \fRdate\fP is an optional key, it is required to be present whenever \fRtime\fP is present\&.
